x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$  has an infinite number of nonzero solutions.\n- **II.** The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly dependent.\n- **III.** The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent.\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139535468 17592186045443 true] [17592186120318 1074 "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 7{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we conclude that the basic variables are ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$, while ${x}_{3}$ is the free variable. \n\nSince there is one free variable, the equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has an infinite number of nonzero solutions, which means that the vectors are linearly dependent. \n\nTherefore, statement III is false, while statements I and II are true." 13194139535468 17592186045443 false] [17592186120318 1074 "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 7{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we conclude that the basic variables are ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$, while ${x}_{3}$ is the free variable. \n\nSince there is one free variable, the equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has an infinite number of nonzero solutions, which means that the vectors are linearly dependent. \n\nTherefore, statement III is false, while statements I and II are true." 13194139535468 17592186045443 true] [17592186120321 1071 "The set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is linearly dependent. Which of the following vectors could be ${a}_{2}$ if\n${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]?$" 13194139535468 17592186045443 false] [17592186120321 1071 "The set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is linearly dependent. Which of the following vectors could be ${a}_{2}$ if\n${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}?$" 13194139535468 17592186045443 true] [17592186120321 1074 "We say that two vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nA set containing *only two* vectors is linearly *dependent* if and only if the two vectors are parallel.\n\nFrom the given options, the correct answer is \n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -20 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo see why, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -20 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]=5[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=5{a}_{1}.\n$$\n\nThis way, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{a}_{1}-5{a}_{1} & =0 \\\\ 5{a}_{1}-{a}_{2} & =0 \\\\ 5{a}_{1}+(-1){a}_{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are parallel.\n\nTo see why this means that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly dependent, notice that the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nhas the solution ${x}_{1}=5$ and ${x}_{2}=-1,$ which is different from ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the set is linearly dependent." 13194139535468 17592186045443 false] [17592186120321 1074 "We say that two vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nA set containing *only two* vectors is linearly *dependent* if and only if the two vectors are parallel.\n\nFrom the given options, the correct answer is \n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -20 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo see why, notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -20 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}=5\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=5{a}_{1}.\n$$\n\nThis way, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{a}_{1}-5{a}_{1} & =0 \\\\ 5{a}_{1}-{a}_{2} & =0 \\\\ 5{a}_{1}+(-1){a}_{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are parallel.\n\nTo see why this means that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly dependent, notice that the equa