ector $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2{b}_{1}-3{b}_{2} \\\\  & =2(\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{1}+\\frac{2}{3}{c}_{2})-3(-\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{1}+\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{c}_{1}+\\frac{4}{3}{c}_{2}+{c}_{1}-{c}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}{c}_{1}+\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139535468 17592186045443 false] [17592186120532 1074 "We are given that\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}={b}_{1}-2{b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2}={b}_{1}+{b}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we express the $b$'s in terms of $c$'s. Multiplying the second equation by $2$ and adding the equations, we get \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2}=3{b}_{1}\\,⟹\\,{b}_{1}=\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{1}+\\frac{2}{3}{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=(\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{1}+\\frac{2}{3}{c}_{2})-2{b}_{2}\\,⟹\\,{b}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{1}+\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nSince $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$, we can write the vector $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2{b}_{1}-3{b}_{2} \\\\  & =2(\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{1}+\\frac{2}{3}{c}_{2})-3(-\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{1}+\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{c}_{1}+\\frac{4}{3}{c}_{2}+{c}_{1}-{c}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}{c}_{1}+\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.$" 13194139535468 17592186045443 true] [17592186121017 1064 "Recall that a matrix is square if the number of rows equals the number of columns. There are other types of square matrices with special names.\n\n- An **upper triangular** matrix is a square matrix in which all entries *below* the diagonal are zeros. For example, the following matrices are all upper triangular matrices:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNote that we can have any number (including $0$) on the diagonal and above.\n\n- A **lower triangular** matrix is a square matrix in which all entries *above* the diagonal are zeros. For example, the following matrices are all lower triangular matrices:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nAgain, note that we can have any number (including $0$) on the diagonal and below." 13194139535468 17592186045443 false] [17592186121017 1064 "Recall that a matrix is square if the number of rows equals the number of columns. There are other types of square matrices with special names.\n\n- An **upper triangular** matrix is a square matrix in which all entries *below* the diagonal are zeros. For example, the following matrices are all upper triangular matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNote that we can have any number (including $0$) on the diagonal and above.\n\n- A **lower triangular** matrix is a square matrix in which all entries *above* the diagonal are zeros. For example, the following matrices are all lower triangular matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nAgain, note that we can have any number (including $0$) on the diagonal and below." 13194139535468 17592186045443 true] [17592186121018 1064 "Sometimes there are lots of calculations involved in finding the determinant of a matrix using a cofactor expansion across the first row. \n\nFortunately, however, we don't always have to use the first row! We can use *any* row we like. A cofactor expansion across any general row is known as a **Laplace expansion**.\n\nAcross the row $i,$ the Laplace expansion of an $n\\!\\times\\! n$ matrix $A$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & ={\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{a}_{ij}{C}_{ij} \\\\  & ={a}_{i1}{C}_{i1}+{a}_{i2}{C}_{i2}+⋯+{a}_{in}{C}_{in},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\color{blue}C_{ij}}$ is the cofactor of the entry $\\color{red}a_{ij}$ in $A.$ Remember the cofactor of the entry $\\color{red}a_{ij}$ is \n\n$$\n{\\color{black}C_{ij}}=(-1)^{i+j}{\\color{black}M_{ij}},\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\color{black}M_{ij}}$ is the minor of the entry.\n\nFor example, consider the following matrix:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo compute the determinant of the matrix above, it is most convenient to use a Laplace expansion along the row with the most zeros. So, let's use Laplace expansion across the third row ($i=3$) as follows:\n\n![](images/6e/6e8d5f7f4111e8963a6d4c7daf2bd5116d8099e18f41c53f2dae73f659f01e35.svg)\n\nSo, we reduced a $4 \\times 4$ determinant to a $3 \\times 3$ determinant." 13194139535468 17592186045443 false] [17592186121018 1064 "Sometimes there are lots of calculations involved in finding the determinant of a matrix using a cofactor expansion across the first row. \n\nFortunately, however, we don't always have to use the first row! We can use *any* row we like. A cofactor expansion across any general row is known as a **Laplace expansion**.\n\nAcross the row $i,$ the Laplace expansion of an $n\\!\\times\\! n$ matrix $A$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & ={\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{a}_{ij}{C}_{ij} \\\\  & ={a}_{i1}{C}_{i1}+{a}_{i2}{C}_{i2}+⋯+{a}_{in}{C}_{in},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\color{blue}C_{ij}}$ is the cofactor of the entry $\\color{red}a_{ij}$ in $A.$ Remember the cofactor of the entry $\\color{red}a_{ij}$ is \n\n$$\n{\\color{black}C_{ij}}=(-1)^{i+j}{\\color{black}M_{ij}},\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\color{black}M_{ij}}$ is the minor of the entry.\n\nFor example, consider the following matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo compute the determinant of the matrix above, it is most convenient to use a Laplace expansion along the row with the most zeros. So, let's use Laplace expansion across the third row ($i=3$) as follows:\n\n![](images/6e/6e8d5f7f4111e8963a6d4c7daf2bd5116d8099e18f41c53f2dae73f659f01e35.svg)\n\nSo, we reduced a $4 \\times 4$ determinant to a $3 \\times 3$ determinant." 13194139535468 17592186045443 true] [17592186121024 1064 "One final important property of the determinant is that it is **linear in each row and column**. For rows, for example, this means \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & det[\\begin{aligned}⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ {◻}_{1}+{△}_{1} & {◻}_{2}+{△}_{2} & … & {◻}_{n}+{△}_{n} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\,\\,=det[\\begin{aligned}⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ {◻}_{1} & {◻}_{2} & … & {◻}_{n} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮\\end{aligned}]+det[\\begin{aligned}⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ {△}_{1} & {△}_{2} & … & {△}_{n} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor instance, it's easy to check that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{-2}{\\overset{⏞}{det[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]}} & =det[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0+3 & 4+0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{det[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{4}+{{det[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{-6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that this property also holds for columns:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det[\\begin{aligned}⋯ & {◻}_{1}+{△}_{1} & ⋯ \\\\ ⋯ & {◻}_{2}+{△}_{2} & ⋯ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ ⋯ & {◻}_{n}+{△}_{n} & ⋯\\end{aligned}]=det[\\begin{aligned}⋯ & {◻}_{1} & ⋯ \\\\ ⋯ & {◻}_{2} & ⋯ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ ⋯ & {◻}_{n} & ⋯\\end{aligned}]+det[\\begin{aligned}⋯ & {△}_{1} & ⋯ \\\\ ⋯ & {△}_{2} & ⋯ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ ⋯ & {△}_{n} & ⋯\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor example, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{-2}{\\overset{⏞}{det[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]}} & =det[\\begin{aligned}1+0 & 2 \\\\ 0+3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{det[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{4}+{{det[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{-6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535468 17592186045443 false] [17592186121024 1064 "One final important property of the determinant is that it is **linear in each row and column**. For rows, for example, this means \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & det\\begin{bmatrix}⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ {◻}_{1}+{△}_{1} & {◻}_{2}+{△}_{2} & … & {◻}_{n}+{△}_{n} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & \\,\\,=det\\begin{bmatrix}⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ {◻}_{1} & {◻}_{2} & … & {◻}_{n} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮\\end{bmatrix}+det\\begin{bmatrix}⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ {△}_{1} & {△}_{2} & … & {△}_{n} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor instance, it's easy to check that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{-2}{\\overset{⏞}{det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}} & =det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0+3 & 4+0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{4}+{{det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{-6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that this property also holds for columns:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det\\begin{bmatrix}⋯ & {◻}_{1}+{△}_{1} & ⋯ \\\\ ⋯ & {◻}_{2}+{△}_{2} & ⋯ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ ⋯ & {◻}_{n}+{△}_{n} & ⋯\\end{bmatrix}=det\\begin{bmatrix}⋯ & {◻}_{1} & ⋯ \\\\ ⋯ & {◻}_{2} & ⋯ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ ⋯ & {◻}_{n} & ⋯\\end{bmatrix}+det\\begin{bmatrix}⋯ & {△}_{1} & ⋯ \\\\ ⋯ & {△}_{2} & ⋯ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ ⋯ & {△}_{n} & ⋯\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor example, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{-2}{\\overset{⏞}{det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}} & =det\\begin{bmatrix}1+0 & 2 \\\\ 0+3 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{4}+{{det\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{-6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535468 17592186045443 true] [17592186121025 1064 "The minor of the entry $a_{ij}$ in a $n\\times n$ matrix is the determinant that we get when the rows and columns containing $a_{ij}$ are removed.\n\nFor example, suppose that we wish to find the minor of $a_{32}$ in the following matrix:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 5 & 12 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 1 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo get the minor of $a_{\\color{red}{3}\\color{blue}{2}}$, we remove the $\\color{red}3$rd row and $\\color{blue}2$nd column:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & ∗ & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & ∗ & 5 & 12 \\\\ ∗ & -2 & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ -5 & ∗ & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSo, the minor of $a_{32}$, denoted $M_{32}$, is \n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 5 & 12 \\\\ -5 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}|\n$$\n\nWe can now calculate the value of $M_{32}$ by evaluating the determinant using previously discussed methods." 13194139535468 17592186045443 false] [17592186121025 1064 "The minor of the entry $a_{ij}$ in a $n\\times n$ matrix is the determinant that we get when the rows and columns containing $a_{ij}$ are removed.\n\nFor example, suppose that we wish to find the minor of $a_{32}$ in the following matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 5 & 12 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 1 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo get the minor of $a_{\\color{red}{3}\\color{blue}{2}}$, we remove the $\\color{red}3$rd row and $\\color{blue}2$nd column:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & ∗ & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & ∗ & 5 & 12 \\\\ ∗ & -2 & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ -5 & ∗ & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, the minor of $a_{32}$, denoted $M_{32}$, is \n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 5 & 12 \\\\ -5 & 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe can now calculate the value of $M_{32}$ by evaluating the determinant using previously discussed methods." 13194139535468 17592186045443 true] [17592186121028 1064 "In the same way that we can factor numbers or polynomials, we can factor a matrix by writing it as a product of two or more matrices. In fact, there is one factorization technique that works for *all* matrices, called **LU factorization** (or **LU decomposition**).\n\nLU factorization involves writing a matrix $A$ as a product of two matrices $L$ and $U,$ where\n\n- ![](images/ad/adca162da15cab5989e5726acb495fc12533231c2e38b0392d68388aeb18493b.svg) is a *unit lower triangular matrix* (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal), and\n\n- ![](images/93/93338df4d1ee1791dc5b1679dbddff34d94d05677c362b1a41dcb925ca83a1dc.svg) is an *upper triangular matrix*.\n\nFor example, we can write the matrix ![](images/83/835b8ad372998cbb35be4c1998d236fe96f1d1b03ee1ad7062bdbbb77e254bf6.svg) in LU factored form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -6\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nYou may be wondering how we arrived at this factorization. Shortly, we'll discuss an algorithm that explains how to compute $L$ and $U$ for any $2\\times2$ matrix $A.$ But for now, let's focus on recognizing LU factored forms." 13194139535468 17592186045443 false] [17592186121028 1064 "In the same way that we can factor numbers or polynomials, we can factor a matrix by writing it as a product of two or more matrices. In fact, there is one factorization technique that works for *all* matrices, called **LU factorization** (or **LU decomposition**).\n\nLU factorization involves writing a matrix $A$ as a product of two matrices $L$ and $U,$ where\n\n- ![](images/ad/adca162da15cab5989e5726acb495fc12533231c2e38b0392d68388aeb18493b.svg) is a *unit lower triangular matrix* (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal), and\n\n- ![](images/93/93338df4d1ee1791dc5b1679dbddff34d94d05677c362b1a41dcb925ca83a1dc.svg) is an *upper triangular matrix*.\n\nFor example, we can write the matrix ![](images/83/835b8ad372998cbb35be4c1998d236fe96f1d1b03ee1ad7062bdbbb77e254bf6.svg) in LU factored form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -6\\end{bmatrix}={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nYou may be wondering how we arrived at this factorization. Shortly, we'll discuss an algorithm that explains how to compute $L$ and $U$ for any $2\\times2$ matrix $A.$ But for now, let's focus on recognizing LU factored forms." 13194139535468 17592186045443 true] [17592186121029 1064 "Recall that LU factorization involves writing a matrix $A$ as a product of two matrices $L$ and $U,$ where\n\n- ![](images/2e/2e6c0b6fdaf90e3a3e23ed91ffe09cb360bb39325b9a630f1b7219b3188a25db.svg) is a *unit lower triangular matrix* (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal), and\n\n- ![](images/0c/0c5d7337e47a53dfa5bdce8c6f4a96bb1e52ef6054349011a6cc38f29701cc61.svg) is an *upper triangular matrix*.\n\nFor example, suppose we're given the matrix ![](images/22/22dba4fe66ac5478f5d81a68342a01809a360de8e722e809998655e7740bafe8.svg) To perform LU factorization, we need to write $A$ as \n\n![](images/d4/d4c92b2e8252619f9a1511d166be884b9a2bdb4311eea8e408e8fe75eed6f36c.svg)\n\nAs usual, to find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we can reduce the matrix $A$ to *echelon form* while simultaneously performing the same elementary row operations on the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 7\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} & ⇔ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]=U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since $E_1A=U,$ we can invert $E_1$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Caution:** In order to get a unit lower triangular matrix at the end, we have the following restriction while reducing a matrix $A$ to its *echelon form*:\n\n- we can only add multiples of higher rows to lower rows ($R_j:=R_j+k R_i$ where $i < j$), and\n\n- the other elementary row operations (swapping or multiplying a row by a number) are not allowed." 13194139535468 17592186045443 false] [17592186121029 1064 "Recall that LU factorization involves writing a matrix $A$ as a product of two matrices $L$ and $U,$ where\n\n- ![](images/2e/2e6c0b6fdaf90e3a3e23ed91ffe09cb360bb39325b9a630f1b7219b3188a25db.svg) is a *unit lower triangular matrix* (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal), and\n\n- ![](images/0c/0c5d7337e47a53dfa5bdce8c6f4a96bb1e52ef6054349011a6cc38f29701cc61.svg) is an *upper triangular matrix*.\n\nFor example, suppose we're given the matrix ![](images/22/22dba4fe66ac5478f5d81a68342a01809a360de8e722e809998655e7740bafe8.svg) To perform LU factorization, we need to write $A$ as \n\n![](images/d4/d4c92b2e8252619f9a1511d166be884b9a2bdb4311eea8e408e8fe75eed6f36c.svg)\n\nAs usual, to find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we can reduce the matrix $A$ to *echelon form* while simultaneously performing the same elementary row operations on the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 7\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} & ⇔ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}=U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since $E_1A=U,$ we can invert $E_1$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Caution:** In order to get a unit lower triangular matrix at the end, we have the following restriction while reducing a matrix $A$ to its *echelon form*:\n\n- we can only add multiples of higher rows to lower rows ($R_j:=R_j+k R_i$ where $i < j$), and\n\n- the other elementary row operations (swapping or multiplying a row by a number) are not allowed." 13194139535468 17592186045443 true] [17592186121035 1064 "Given two vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2,$ the set of all possible linear combinations of $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2$ is\n\n$$\nU = \\left\\{ x_1 \\mathbf{a}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{a}_2 \\: : \\: x_1,x_2\\in\\mathbb{R} \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nThe set $U$ is also called the **linear span** of $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2,$ and is denoted by $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\}.$ We say that $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2$ are the **generators** of $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\}.$\n\nFor example, let's find the linear span generated by the following vectors:\n![](images/55/55c749dccc54c252b9120f148e383f395c09fb4cd4bec0e1cce8ed441ecb6199.svg)\n\nFrom the definition of linear span, we know that if $\\mathbf{b} \\in \\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\},$ then $\\mathbf{b}$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{b} = x_1\\mathbf{a_1} + x_2\\mathbf{a_2}\n$$\n\n for some $x_1,x_2\\in\\mathbb{R}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & ={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{1} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{2} \\\\ 2{x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the linear span of $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2$ is\n![](images/21/2181b471f34906eeaea7889abbba37c797a2f9624cee455b43b00fc048247cd1.svg)\n\nGeometrically, $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\}$ can be viewed as the plane $\\Pi$ that passes through the origin and is parallel to the vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/d5/d5b202475e0bc3ebb07df1f07bab72e9e74b13a99196c2a8a5adb624a7534a03.png)" 13194139535468 17592186045443 false] [17592186121035 1064 "Given two vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2,$ the set of all possible linear combinations of $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2$ is\n\n$$\nU = \\left\\{ x_1 \\mathbf{a}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{a}_2 \\: : \\: x_1,x_2\\in\\mathbb{R} \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nThe set $U$ is also called the **linear span** of $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2,$ and is denoted by $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\}.$ We say that $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2$ are the **generators** of $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\}.$\n\nFor example, let's find the linear span generated by the following vectors:\n![](images/55/55c749dccc54c252b9120f148e383f395c09fb4cd4bec0e1cce8ed441ecb6199.svg)\n\nFrom the definition of linear span, we know that if $\\mathbf{b} \\in \\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\},$ then $\\mathbf{b}$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{b} = x_1\\mathbf{a_1} + x_2\\mathbf{a_2}\n$$\n\n for some $x_1,x_2\\in\\mathbb{R}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & ={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{1} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}_{2} \\\\ 2{x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the linear span of $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2$ is\n![](images/21/2181b471f34906eeaea7889abbba37c797a2f9624cee455b43b00fc048247cd1.svg)\n\nGeometrically, $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\}$ can be viewed as the plane $\\Pi$ that passes through the origin and is parallel to the vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/d5/d5b202475e0bc3ebb07df1f07bab72e9e74b13a99196c2a8a5adb624a7534a03.png)" 13194139535468 17592186045443 true] [17592186121037 1064 "The **column space** of a matrix $A$ is denoted $\\text{Col}(A)$ and is the span of the columns of $A$ (i.e., the set of all possible linear combinations of columns of $A$). For example, consider the matrix\n\n![](images/6c/6c03ab3dfd7b75c9a89defe102f33946da200fb88d26139e54c889a90467068a.svg)\nwhere we denote its columns by $\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}_1$ and ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2},$ respectively. The column space of this matrix is given by\n![](images/5f/5faad23bc77f821d42223beaca06364e38c7f0ba8a4869cb138e0e923e90b8f7.svg)\n\nNow, suppose we would like to determine whether the vector ![](images/45/45349edee356334c54ad85a6d60757a6a7833a392962086815a2436446e323dc.svg) lies in $\\textrm{Col}(A).$ In other words, we need to check if $\\mathbf{b} \\in \\textrm{Span}\\{ {\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}_1}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2} \\}.$ So, we need to determine whether $\\mathbf{b}$ can be written as some linear combination of $\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}_1$ and ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the equation above can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =b \\\\ {{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}{{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{x}} & ={{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{b}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation is equivalent to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}.$ To solve the equation, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[A \\,|\\, \\mathbf{b}]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,b] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -7 & -7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=2 \\\\ -7{x}_{2}=-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $x_2=1.$ Substituting into the first equation, we get $x_1=-1.$\n\nSince we have a solution, the equation $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ is consistent, which implies that $\\mathbf{b} \\in \\textrm{Col}(A).$\n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $\\mathbf{b}$ as a linear combination of $\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}_1$ and ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2}\\mathbin{:}$\n![](images/a3/a3965a53b3b3cfda2e11c4a68ba882631dc2763f32de67de30b4751aca76f2e5.svg)" 13194139535468 17592186045443 false] [17592186121037 1064 "The **column space** of a matrix $A$ is denoted $\\text{Col}(A)$ and is the span of the columns of $A$ (i.e., the set of all possible linear combinations of columns of $A$). For example, consider the matrix\n\n![](images/6c/6c03ab3dfd7b75c9a89defe102f33946da200fb88d26139e54c889a90467068a.svg)\nwhere we denote its columns by $\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}_1$ and ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2},$ respectively. The column space of this matrix is given by\n![](images/5f/5faad23bc77f821d42223beaca06364e38c7f0ba8a4869cb138e0e923e90b8f7.svg)\n\nNow, suppose we would like to determine whether the vector ![](images/45/45349edee356334c54ad85a6d60757a6a7833a392962086815a2436446e323dc.svg) lies in $\\textrm{Col}(A).$ In other words, we need to check if $\\mathbf{b} \\in \\textrm{Span}\\{ {\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}_1}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2} \\}.$ So, we need to determine whether $\\mathbf{b}$ can be written as some linear combination of $\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}_1$ and ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the equation above can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} & {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =b \\\\ {{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}{{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{x}} & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{b}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation is equivalent to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}.$ To solve the equation, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[A \\,|\\, \\mathbf{b}]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,b] & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -7 & -7\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=2 \\\\ -7{x}_{2}=-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $x_2=1.$ Substituting into the first equation, we get $x_1=-1.$\n\nSince we have a solution, the equation $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ is consistent, which implies that $\\mathbf{b} \\in \\textrm{Col}(A).$\n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $\\mathbf{b}$ as a linear combination of $\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}_1$ and ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2}\\mathbin{:}$\n![](images/a3/a3965a53b3b3cfda2e11c4a68ba882631dc2763f32de67de30b4751aca76f2e5.svg)" 13194139535468 17592186045443 true] [17592186121039 1064 "The **null space** of a matrix $A$ is the set of all the solutions of the equation $A\\mathbf{x}= \\mathbf{0},$ and is denoted by $\\textrm{Null}(A).$ \n\nTo illustrate, let's consider the following matrix:\n\n![](images/c3/c3bf583e98daad3fa753880b96c6ba85bce697f3434690977c5679b64ffd4882.svg)\n\nThe null space consists of all the solutions of $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.$ For example, the vectors ![](images/9d/9d94e47625ee3c9d6fdff788db07e925ec2375e6c57cc6c1d8897ba423749abf.svg) ![](images/e5/e5a5f6b93eccdf25da72ea39af64a4a5cca7b0a03d5f469cb85554dc6dc12f88.svg) and $[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ all satisfy the equation $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0},$ so they are all elements of the null space:\n\n![](images/5e/5ededa27ee77302b18683f91e37d854a68cd3ba6ceacc7e2a6068a2730b4a9bf.svg)" 13194139535468 17592186045443 false] [17592186121039 1064 "The **null space** of a matrix $A$ is the set of all the solutions of the equation $A\\mathbf{x}= \\mathbf{0},$ and is denoted by $\\textrm{Null}(A).$ \n\nTo illustrate, let's consider the following matrix:\n\n![](images/c3/c3bf583e98daad3fa753880b96c6ba85bce697f3434690977c5679b64ffd4882.svg)\n\nThe null space consists of all the solutions of $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.$ For example, the vectors ![](images/9d/9d94e47625ee3c9d6fdff788db07e925ec2375e6c57cc6c1d8897ba423749abf.svg) ![](images/e5/e5a5f6b93eccdf25da72ea39af64a4a5cca7b0a03d5f469cb85554dc6dc12f88.svg) and $\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ all satisfy the equation $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0},$ so they are all elements of the null space:\n\n![](images/5e/5ededa27ee77302b18683f91e37d854a68cd3ba6ceacc7e2a6068a2730b4a9bf.svg)" 13194139535468 17592186045443 true] [17592186121041 1064 "Remember that the null space $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ of a matrix $A$ consists of the solutions to the equation $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}.$ To find a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A),$ we solve the equation $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}$ and express the solution as a linear combination of linearly independent vectors. Those vectors form a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A).$\n\nTo illustrate, consider the matrix ![](images/70/702794c0129df672cc7c4cc65260c9bfa7e33804ea9f71bba048cf62142a0a18.svg) Computing the reduced row echelon form of $A,$ we get\n\n![](images/16/16d52c068c616e2eca149a8e81929a98c85726361d7ea2291ab314fe7567e4ce.svg)\n\nSo, the equation $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}$ is equivalent to the system\n\n![](images/bb/bb8743bd8eb072476af0e9e31c0215916beeeb82fc371348de1e6013b38810f8.svg)\n\nwhere $\\color{blue}x_1$ and $\\color{blue}x_2$ are basic variables (corresponding to the pivot columns) and $\\color{red}x_3$ is a free variable (corresponding to the non-pivot column).\n\nTherefore, any vector $\\mathbf{x}$ in $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ has the form\n\n![](images/6e/6e124e2c030f6cad72932d09211935b438f1189322ded40683a1f18618e35f3e.svg)\n\nSo, the set ![](images/24/2449d6abf866e2baa123e81c1d610940930c75c6b45ecf818d36c62191ef605f.svg) is a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ since it is a linearly independent set and the vector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ generates the null space of $A.$\n\n**Note:** It might be the case that $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ for a particular matrix $A$ equals the so-called zero subspace $\\{ \\mathbf{0} \\}$ containing only the zero-vector. The basis of $\\{ \\mathbf{0} \\}$ is said to be the *empty set*, which is a set that does not contain any vectors. The empty set is denoted by $\\emptyset$ or $\\{\\,\\}.$" 13194139535468 17592186045443 false] [17592186121041 1064 "Remember that the null space $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ of a matrix $A$ consists of the solutions to the equation $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}.$ To find a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A),$ we solve the equation $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}$ and express the solution as a linear combination of linearly independent vectors. Those vectors form a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A).$\n\nTo illustrate, consider the matrix ![](images/70/702794c0129df672cc7c4cc65260c9bfa7e33804ea9f71bba048cf62142a0a18.svg) Computing the reduced row echelon form of $A,$ we get\n\n![](images/16/16d52c068c616e2eca149a8e81929a98c85726361d7ea2291ab314fe7567e4ce.svg)\n\nSo, the equation $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}$ is equivalent to the system\n\n![](images/bb/bb8743bd8eb072476af0e9e31c0215916beeeb82fc371348de1e6013b38810f8.svg)\n\nwhere $\\color{blue}x_1$ and $\\color{blue}x_2$ are basic variables (corresponding to the pivot columns) and $\\color{red}x_3$ is a free variable (corresponding to the non-pivot column).\n\nTherefore, any vector $\\mathbf{x}$ in $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ has the form\n\n![](images/6e/6e124e2c030f6cad72932d0921193