begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's solve for these coefficients. From the above, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=8 \\\\ 2{x}_{2}=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe unique solution of this system is \n\n![](images/82/82d4f494936d23e9b697aa4518edf68f7ea62e504d9d2576a6d13971acaf1c96.svg)\n\nTherefore, we have that \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} = {\\color{red}3} \\mathbf{b}_1+ {\\color{blue}2} \\mathbf{b}_2,\n$$\n\nwhich can be visualized as shown below.\n\n![](images/94/94b2934727a32284e9790e59ccd301bd858a0258843314a2968285a5eda7a302.png)" 13194139535468 17592186045443 false] [17592186121042 1064 "Given a subspace $H$ with a basis $\\mathcal B = \\{ \\mathbf b_1, \\mathbf b_2 \\}$ and a vector $\\mathbf x \\in H,$ the **coordinates of the vector** $\\mathbf{x}$ **relative to the basis** $\\mathcal{B}$ are the coefficients ${\\color{red} x_1}$ and ${\\color{blue} x_2}$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}x_1} \\mathbf{b}_1+{\\color{blue}x_2} \\mathbf{b}_2 = \\mathbf{x}.\n$$\n\nWe can write the vector $\\mathbf x$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal B$ using these coordinates, as follows:\n\n![](images/05/0593ca6a838b66c4623e8749eb5236d6259b1f9bf36ed0d68b83c49781076341.svg)\n\nTo demonstrate, consider the basis $\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2 \\},$ where \n\n![](images/f3/f355faa0c477775f2a1cbb3522d0743cdbc9649a5ee9c55b579b1fac9ae2a661.svg)\n\nAlso let $H = \\textrm{span} \\{ \\mathbf b_1, \\mathbf b_2 \\},$ and consider the vector ![](images/78/78357fcf51c9cd6b7873f5bc35fcc214cb3790bcec00ee69d0932f15aacd39cb.svg)\n\nThe vector $\\mathbf x$ can be expressed as a unique linear combination of $\\mathbf b_1$ and $\\mathbf b_2.$ That is to say, there exist *unique coefficients* $\\color{red}x_1$ and $\\color{blue}x_2$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2} & =x \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's solve for these coefficients. From the above, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=8 \\\\ 2{x}_{2}=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe unique solution of this system is \n\n![](images/82/82d4f494936d23e9b697aa4518edf68f7ea62e504d9d2576a6d13971acaf1c96.svg)\n\nTherefore, we have that \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} = {\\color{red}3} \\mathbf{b}_1+ {\\color{blue}2} \\mathbf{b}_2,\n$$\n\nwhich can be visualized as shown below.\n\n![](images/94/94b2934727a32284e9790e59ccd301bd858a0258843314a2968285a5eda7a302.png)" 13194139535468 17592186045443 true] [17592186121044 1064 "As we saw, swapping two rows does not preserve the determinant. However, the only difference is the sign of the determinant changes. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} & =1\\cdot 4-2\\cdot 3=-2 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & =3\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 4=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in the above example, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} & =-\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, swapping any two rows of a matrix changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite one. That is to say, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det\\begin{bmatrix}⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ ◻ & ◻ & … & ◻ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ △ & △ & … & △ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮\\end{bmatrix}=-det\\begin{bmatrix}⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ △ & △ & … & △ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ ◻ & ◻ & … & ◻ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535468 17592186045443 true] [17592186121044 1064 "As we saw, swapping two rows does not preserve the determinant. However, the only difference is the sign of the determinant changes. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}| & =1\\cdot 4-2\\cdot 3=-2 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| & =3\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 4=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in the above example, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}| & =-|\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, swapping any two rows of a matrix changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite one. That is to say, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det[\\begin{aligned}⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ ◻ & ◻ & … & ◻ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ △ & △ & … & △ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮\\end{aligned}]=-det[\\begin{aligned}⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ △ & △ & … & △ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ ◻ & ◻ & … & ◻ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535468 17592186045443 false] [17592186121045 1064 "As we have seen, multiplying a row by a number does not preserve the determinant. However, it does affect the determinant in a predictable way: multiplying a row by a number scales the determinant by that number as well.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} & =1\\cdot 4-2\\cdot 3=-2 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}2\\cdot 1 & 2\\cdot 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix}=\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} & =2\\cdot 4-3\\cdot 4=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in the above example, we have\n![](images/08/0826c05fe2eabec96065eb2c83228168129b42c309764df4b101f4a520c4a0f9.svg)\n\nIn general, multiplying a row of a matrix by a number multiplies the determinant by that number. That is to say, we can factor out the common multiplier from the row of the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det\\begin{bmatrix}⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ k△ & k△ & … & k△ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮\\end{bmatrix}=k\\cdot det\\begin{bmatrix}⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ △ & △ & … & △ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535468 17592186045443 true] [17592186121045 1064 "As we have seen, multiplying a row by a number does not preserve the determinant. However, it does affect the determinant in a predictable way: multiplying a row by a number scales the determinant by that number as well.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}| & =1\\cdot 4-2\\cdot 3=-2 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot 1 & 2\\cdot 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}|=|\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}| & =2\\cdot 4-3\\cdot 4=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in the above example, we have\n![](images/08/0826c05fe2eabec96065eb2c83228168129b42c309764df4b101f4a520c4a0f9.svg)\n\nIn general, multiplying a row of a matrix by a number multiplies the determinant by that number. That is to say, we can factor out the common multiplier from the row of the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det[\\begin{aligned}⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ k△ & k△ & … & k△ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮\\end{aligned}]=k\\cdot det[\\begin{aligned}⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ △ & △ & … & △ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535468 17592186045443 false] [17592186121048 1064 "Suppose we know that the vector equation of the line $l$ is given by\n![](images/92/92c5ed55727717320b9c128adf1a6d786666adb38cd6ba71d57917e60d661fd8.svg)\n\nand we want to rewrite it using Cartesian coordinates. In other words, we want to create an equation that relates $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ to each other.\n\nWe can do this using the following $3$ steps.\n\n**Step 1:** Find the parametric equations for the line.\n\nSubstituting ![](images/67/67b8360f90540ffb75d39717c375f0ef6e9a8a39cae5832c78957105e0d148b6.svg) into the equation of $l,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3+6t \\\\ -2+5t \\\\ 5-t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=3+6t \\\\ y=-2+5t \\\\ z=5-t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Solve for $t$ in each of the parametric equations.\n\n![](images/ee/eed0a210b121af73bc2ceab04b95def59239a742055284e706a1532b47edc60d.svg)\n\n**Step 3:** Equate the resulting expressions. Because the right-hand side of each equation is equal to $t,$ we can write this system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-3}{6}=\\dfrac{y+2}{5}=\\dfrac{z-5}{-1} = t\n$$\n\nWe can simplify further by omitting the \"$=t$\" on the right-hand side, which gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-3}{6}=\\dfrac{y+2}{5}=\\dfrac{z-5}{-1} \\,.\n$$\n\nAnd we are done! \n\nThis way of writing the equation of a line is called the **Cartesian equation** or **Cartesian canonical equation** (or simply **canonical equation**).\n\nIn general, the Cartesian equation of the straight line that passes through the point $P(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ and is parallel to the vector $\\mathbf{v}=\\langle v_x, v_y, v_z \\rangle$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-x_0}{v_x}=\\dfrac{y-y_0}{v_y}=\\dfrac{z-z_0}{v_z} = t, \\qquad t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).\n$$" 13194139535468 17592186045443 false] [17592186121048 1064 "Suppose we know that the vector equation of the line $l$ is given by\n![](images/92/92c5ed55727717320b9c128adf1a6d786666adb38cd6ba71d57917e60d661fd8.svg)\n\nand we want to rewrite it using Cartesian coordinates. In other words, we want to create an equation that relates $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ to each other.\n\nWe can do this using the following $3$ steps.\n\n**Step 1:** Find the parametric equations for the line.\n\nSubstituting ![](images/67/67b8360f90540ffb75d39717c375f0ef6e9a8a39cae5832c78957105e0d148b6.svg) into the equation of $l,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3+6t \\\\ -2+5t \\\\ 5-t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=3+6t \\\\ y=-2+5t \\\\ z=5-t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Solve for $t$ in each of the parametric equations.\n\n![](images/ee/eed0a210b121af73bc2ceab04b95def59239a742055284e706a1532b47edc60d.svg)\n\n**Step 3:** Equate the resulting expressions. Because the right-hand side of each equation is equal to $t,$ we can write this system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-3}{6}=\\dfrac{y+2}{5}=\\dfrac{z-5}{-1} = t\n$$\n\nWe can simplify further by omitting the \"$=t$\" on the right-hand side, which gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-3}{6}=\\dfrac{y+2}{5}=\\dfrac{z-5}{-1} \\,.\n$$\n\nAnd we are done! \n\nThis way of writing the equation of a line is called the **Cartesian equation** or **Cartesian canonical equation** (or simply **canonical equation**).\n\nIn general, the Cartesian equation of the straight line that passes through the point $P(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ and is parallel to the vector $\\mathbf{v}=\\langle v_x, v_y, v_z \\rangle$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-x_0}{v_x}=\\dfrac{y-y_0}{v_y}=\\dfrac{z-z_0}{v_z} = t, \\qquad t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).\n$$" 13194139535468 17592186045443 true] [17592186121056 1064 "There is something interesting about the bases of a vector space, summarized in the theorem below:\n\nIf the vector space $V$ has a basis with $n$ vectors, then any basis of $V$ has exactly $n$ vectors.\n\nThe number $n$ of vectors in any basis of $V$ is called the **dimension of the space** $V$ and is denoted $\\textrm{dim}(V).$\n\nFor example, consider the linear span below:\n![](images/3f/3f4139a1701c74e72ca55b662b1d5c06b02f1d3b6cab9a96b4667439dc196068.svg)\n\nWe know that, in order to find a basis of the span, we start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 & -6 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that the matrix above has two pivot columns: the $1$st and the $3$rd columns. This tells us that\n$\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1,\\mathbf{v}_3 \\}$ is a basis of $H.$\n\nSince $\\mathcal{B}$ consists of two vectors, *any basis* of $H$ will also have *exactly* two vectors. So, we have $\\textrm{dim}