 the diagonal matrix $D,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$\\qquad$ $\\lambda_1=\\lambda_2=4 \\quad$ and $\\quad \\lambda_3=0.$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$\n\n- Consider $\\lambda=4.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & 6 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, $x_2$ and $x_3$ are free variables. From the first equation, we obtain $x_1=x_2+3x_3.$ Hence, the general solution is\n![](images/d0/d035dcaecd0c84608677f292c7e9b1acd0efc1a5bdd1d0ac43b3a9ba3808295f.svg)\nAs a result, we have two linearly independent eigenvectors\n\n$\\qquad$ ![](images/b7/b7079fa1df34dd45f1899f99f83bb4c4d45a378a86a55827cad09355779ca011.svg) and ![](images/fa/fab597dd2894f23ba307ac5dfd631560b02914757454c42f90b27d10ecd85349.svg)\n\n- Consider $\\lambda=0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-0I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]-0[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking for nonzero solutions of $(A-0I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0},$ we obtain the eigenvector \n\n$\\qquad$ ![](images/a2/a2e8e64f0907a900b1078ced3042ebdaade46cdcee461ece7194d16cfb4b4c20.svg)\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the invertible matrix $P$ using our eigenvectors, as follows:\n![](images/1c/1ca56d30833ee1641678bb74c93dba72b459112a0bde385875a958847bc61c6f.svg)" 13194139535468 17592186045443 false] [17592186124410 1074 "Notice that $A=PDP^{-1}$ is a diagonalization of the matrix $A.$ So let's apply the diagonalization algorithm to $A.$\n\n**Step 1.** From the diagonal matrix $D,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$\\qquad$ $\\lambda_1=\\lambda_2=4 \\quad$ and $\\quad \\lambda_3=0.$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$\n\n- Consider $\\lambda=4.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-4\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & 6 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, $x_2$ and $x_3$ are free variables. From the first equation, we obtain $x_1=x_2+3x_3.$ Hence, the general solution is\n![](images/d0/d035dcaecd0c84608677f292c7e9b1acd0efc1a5bdd1d0ac43b3a9ba3808295f.svg)\nAs a result, we have two linearly independent eigenvectors\n\n$\\qquad$ ![](images/b7/b7079fa1df34dd45f1899f99f83bb4c4d45a378a86a55827cad09355779ca011.svg) and ![](images/fa/fab597dd2894f23ba307ac5dfd631560b02914757454c42f90b27d10ecd85349.svg)\n\n- Consider $\\lambda=0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-0I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-0\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking for nonzero solutions of $(A-0I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0},$ we obtain the eigenvector \n\n$\\qquad$ ![](images/a2/a2e8e64f0907a900b1078ced3042ebdaade46cdcee461ece7194d16cfb4b4c20.svg)\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the invertible matrix $P$ using our eigenvectors, as follows:\n![](images/1c/1ca56d30833ee1641678bb74c93dba72b459112a0bde385875a958847bc61c6f.svg)" 13194139535468 17592186045443 true] [17592186124411 1074 "Since $A$ is triangular, the eigenvalues are the entries on the main diagonal. \n\nSo, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda_1=\\lambda_2=3.$ Since we get one repeated eigenvalue, the matrix might not be diagonalizable. To check this, we row-reduce the matrix $A-3I\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has only $1$ pivot column. This implies that we will have only $2-1=1$ linearly independent eigenvector corresponding to the current eigenvalue. Therefore, $A$ is not diagonalizable." 13194139535468 17592186045443 false] [17592186124411 1074 "Since $A$ is triangular, the eigenvalues are the entries on the main diagonal. \n\nSo, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda_1=\\lambda_2=3.$ Since we get one repeated eigenvalue, the matrix might not be diagonalizable. To check this, we row-reduce the matrix $A-3I\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has only $1$ pivot column. This implies that we will have only $2-1=1$ linearly independent eigenvector corresponding to the current eigenvalue. Therefore, $A$ is not diagonalizable." 13194139535468 17592186045443 true] [17592186124412 1074 "From the diagonal matrix ![](images/0e/0e25fe1b46b828e6fac168d1755af7aed9a7c3f657d1a6d6677fd71746b7ce31.svg) we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are $\\lambda_1=5$ and $\\lambda_2=-1.$\n\nSo, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_2=-1.$ \n\nNow, we simply need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda_2=-1.$ Computing $A-(-1)I,$ we get  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the corresponding system of linear equations, we construct the augmented matrix $M$ and reduce it to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). So, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain $x_1= -{2}x_2.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n![](images/54/54dad1c9d747fead6f27dd0e9af7cca434c40a83d56c02c854bb610a1cdc6087.svg)\n\nBy setting $x_2=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n![](images/d2/d28cf572126716928bc9ded7c2646871540e20b1d93d1efc929599b4c379d38b.svg)\n\nFinally, $\\dfrac{a}{b}=\\dfrac{-2}{1} = -2.$" 13194139535468 17592186045443 false] [17592186124412 1074 "From the diagonal matrix ![](images/0e/0e25fe1b46b828e6fac168d1755af7aed9a7c3f657d1a6d6677fd71746b7ce31.svg) we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are $\\lambda_1=5$ and $\\lambda_2=-1.$\n\nSo, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_2=-1.$ \n\nNow, we simply need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda_2=-1.$ Computing $A-(-1)I,$ we get  \n\n$$\n\\